---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('sequenzy')!;
const b = appBySlug('drip')!;
const path = '/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-drip');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/sequenzy-vs-drip');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Sequenzy or Drip for cart recovery?",
    "answer": "Evaluate Sequenzy for connected email sequences and purchase-based segments; evaluate Drip when distinguishing identified cart activity from checkout events is central to your workflow. Choose after a demonstration using your store events and pending-message rules, not a claimed recovery ranking."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are cart and checkout events interchangeable?",
    "answer": "No. Drip documents separate Updated a Cart and Checkout Created/Updated triggers. Cart tracking requires an identified person. Check the corresponding identity and trigger behavior in Sequenzy rather than assuming equivalent event coverage."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will a queued message always show the newest basket?",
    "answer": "Do not assume so. Drip warns that products added after an email is queued will not be included. Test basket changes before and after queueing in each candidate and decide what content is safe."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both tools send recovery messages?",
    "answer": "A migration may require both connections temporarily, but each recovery step needs one active sender. Verify purchase exits, subscription state, pending messages and replay behavior before activating the replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should discounts be compared?",
    "answer": "Subtract the discount from revenue while holding product cost constant. Then include fulfillment, fees, messaging and returns. Compare eligible orders and incremental contribution rather than attributed revenue alone."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much will the replacement cost?",
    "answer": "Price the same contact population, email volume and required features in both current plans. Include implementation, overlap, connectors, any separate channel supplier and ongoing workflow maintenance."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Compare the events that start recovery, the basket a queued email will show, and the work required to move an existing flow safely.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Evaluate Sequenzy', tool: a.name, reason: 'Connected Shopify email sequences and purchase-based segments.' }, { label: 'Evaluate Drip', tool: b.name, reason: 'Explicit cart and checkout trigger choices for identified shoppers.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>The decision starts before the first reminder</h2>
        <p>This comparison is for a Shopify operator choosing the owner of an email recovery flow, especially one moving an existing Drip workflow or considering broader lifecycle sequences in Sequenzy. A polished reminder is useful only if the right person enters with the right basket. Start by distinguishing a visitor adding an item from a shopper reaching checkout, then inspect what happens while a message waits.</p>
        <p>Sequenzy's <a href="https://www.sequenzy.com/features/shopify-integration">Shopify overview</a> describes customer, order and product sync, recovery sequences and purchase-based segmentation. That is a reason to evaluate connected lifecycle email. It does not establish identical trigger semantics or queue behavior to Drip.</p>
        <p>Drip's <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">cart versus checkout event guide</a> distinguishes activity before checkout from checkout events, requires identification for cart tracking, and warns about basket changes after queueing. Sources checked November 24, 2026; these are published capabilities and constraints, not tests conducted by this site.</p>
        <h2>Use the event boundary to choose the demonstration</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Sequenzy evaluation</th><th>Drip evaluation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Recovery before checkout</td><td>Demonstrate which storefront action creates a usable recovery entry and how identity is resolved.</td><td>Inspect Updated a Cart tracking, identification and the integration permissions in the installed store.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Checkout recovery</td><td>Trace a test checkout into the intended sequence with its recovery destination.</td><td>Inspect Checkout Created/Updated entries separately from cart entries.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing customer treatment</td><td>Demonstrate purchase-based segmentation using the synced order history.</td><td>Show the intended branch using the actual customer history and event data.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Basket freshness</td><td>Compare content before and after queueing rather than assuming live rendering.</td><td>Account for the documented exclusion of products added after an email is queued.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Proposed trial: a basket changes on its way to checkout</h2>
        <p>Use designated test recipients and record event time, person identity, basket identifier, entry trigger, queued content and destination. The cases below are proposed acceptance checks. They are not claims of observed conversion lift or verified behavior in either account.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Unidentified visitor:</strong> add an item without an existing recognized identity. Inspect whether any usable recipient exists. Do not interpret a missed anonymous entry as proof that an identified-cart feature is broken.</li>
          <li><strong>Identified shopper:</strong> repeat with a recognized test contact. Confirm the intended entry, then reach checkout. Determine whether two events create competing recovery paths. A shared recipient does not by itself deduplicate workflows.</li>
          <li><strong>Changed basket:</strong> alter quantity or variant before a message queues, then add another product after queueing. Preview the actual pending message and follow its destination. If content is a snapshot, decide whether it remains useful or whether the flow needs a different message or cancellation rule.</li>
          <li><strong>Purchase while waiting:</strong> complete the order and inspect the exit and pending queue. Trace delayed order sync separately from missing exit logic. Do not promise cancellation merely because the order eventually appears on the contact.</li>
          <li><strong>Withdrawn permission:</strong> change the relevant subscription state while work is pending. Inspect dispatch eligibility and any separate welcome flow. Knowing a person's email address is not the same as permission for every message purpose.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeated event:</strong> repeat an update or reconnect the integration. Inspect whether retries produce duplicate entries, replace basket state or restart a journey. Preserve the event identifiers needed to diagnose the result.</li>
        </ol>
        <h2>Configure the existing flow before replacing it</h2>
        <p>For Drip, check whether add-to-cart tracking has been enabled in the Shopify integration and inspect the installed workflow's triggers. The guide distinguishes new template installations from existing workflows that need the cart trigger added. Diagnose configuration before attributing a difference in reach to the platform.</p>
        <p>For Sequenzy, demonstrate the same required path from a store event to a sequence and its purchase-based branch. Record which customer and order fields arrive before the branch runs. A connected account or imported contact list cannot establish that a particular cart becomes a usable recovery message. Use the demonstration to decide whether the broader lifecycle workflow reduces your actual maintenance work.</p>
        <h2>A migration needs queue ownership</h2>
        <p>Inventory active Shopify reminders, Drip workflows, Sequenzy sequences and any separate channel sender. Assign each entry and message to one owner. Keep replacement sends inactive while checking customer history, exclusions, subscription records and recovery destinations. Imported history should not silently enroll old abandoned baskets as new work.</p>
        <p>At cutover, decide whether eligible old messages will drain or be cancelled, then verify that choice in the queue. Keep the former workflow disabled when the replacement activates. For rollback, pause new entries and inspect pending replacement messages before restoring the former sender; otherwise both systems can resume a reminder for the same shopper. Use actual processing and queue duration to plan the transition, rather than a universal two-week overlap.</p>
        <h2>Make the offer decision with unchanged product cost</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical basket example: a $348 basket at 50% product gross margin has $174 product cost and $174 full-price gross profit. A 12% code reduces revenue by $41.76 to $306.24. With unchanged product cost, gross profit is $132.24, a $41.76 reduction. Shipping subsidies, payment fees, messaging costs and returns still need to be deducted. Neither platform has been shown to recover this basket.</p>
        <p>Use an offer only if it addresses the shopper's problem. A stale variant link or unanswered delivery question calls for accurate content or assistance before a discount. If a welcome benefit is already available, inspect checkout eligibility and stacking rules before adding a cart code. Measure the cost of discounting buyers who would have purchased anyway.</p>
        <h2>Measure a replacement against a comparable population</h2>
        <p>Define the eligible group at the same event boundary. A cart-trigger flow can reach a different population from a checkout-only flow, so comparing raw recovered revenue can reward reach rather than better messaging. Reconcile unique orders, refunds, discount cost and overlapping sender attribution. A platform-attributed order is not proof of an incremental sale.</p>
        <p>If evaluating incremental impact, plan a randomized holdout or other defensible experiment using expected eligible volume, the normal purchase cycle and the change worth detecting. Set the evaluation window before reviewing outcomes. Do not prescribe an arbitrary holdout percentage or declare a winner from a small trial.</p>
        <h2>Compare operating cost and make the choice</h2>
        <p>Obtain current quotes for the same contact population, email usage, required automation and support. Include setup, historical-data migration, connectors, overlap subscriptions and ongoing changes to branches and templates. Add any separately required SMS supplier and its usage only if SMS is part of your intended design; this page does not establish equivalent SMS support.</p>
        <p>Keep Drip when its installed event coverage and maintained workflow already meet the requirement and a switch has no demonstrated benefit. Evaluate Sequenzy when its connected sequences and purchase-based segments solve a concrete lifecycle need. Before choosing, require the event and queue demonstrations above and estimate who will maintain them. Neither a fixed star rating nor a generic lifecycle ranking answers that decision.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Sequenzy vs Drip FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
